\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 PyMuPDF exposes page text blocks, words, coordinates, images, links, labels, and OCR hooks, making it unusually good for building your own page map rather than accepting a black-box output. Its docs describe block/word extraction with bounding boxes and page label support ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pymupdf.readthedocs.io/en/latest/app1.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 PyMuPDF text extraction}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pymupdf.readthedocs.io/en/latest/document.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 PyMuPDF page labels}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pymupdf.readthedocs.io/en/latest/recipes-ocr.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 PyMuPDF OCR}}). pdfplumber is valuable because it lets you inspect low-level page objects and debug tables/coordinates, while pypdf is better treated as a PDF utility and simple text extractor rather than your main layout authority ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pypdf.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user/extract-text.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 pypdf extraction docs}}).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Born-Digital PDFs
\f1\b0 \
For born-digital PDFs, your safest path is:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Detect whether selectable text exists.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Extract page text, words, blocks, images, links, labels, and coordinates with PyMuPDF.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cross-check text and table regions with pdfplumber.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Run Docling or Marker only as a structure proposal.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Run GROBID when the document is an academic article, thesis, report with scholarly references, or anything citation-heavy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Born-digital PDFs are still not \'93structured documents.\'94 A PDF often says \'93draw this glyph here,\'94 not \'93this is a paragraph in section 2.1.\'94 So your archive should store both:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \{\
  "page_index": 42,\
  "pdf_page_number": 43,\
  "page_label": "37",\
  "width": 612,\
  "height": 792,\
  "coordinate_origin": "top-left",\
  "text_blocks": [],\
  "words": [],\
  "extraction_method": "pymupdf.get_text('dict')",\
  "source_sha256": "..."\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 This lets you cite page 37 even if it is physical PDF page 43.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Scanned PDFs
\f1\b0 \
For scanned PDFs, use 
\f0\b OCRmyPDF + Tesseract
\f1\b0  first, because it gives you a reproducible, local OCR pass and can create a searchable PDF plus a sidecar text file. OCRmyPDF\'92s sidecar has an important caveat: it may include only OCRed pages, not pages that already had text or pages skipped by options such as timeouts ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ocrmypdf.readthedocs.io/en/v11.3.0/cookbook.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 OCRmyPDF sidecar docs}}). Tesseract can output TSV/hOCR with word-level bounding boxes and confidence scores, which is exactly what you need for provenance and QA ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tesseractocr.org/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Tesseract docs}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/FAQ.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Tesseract output formats}}).\
For scans, store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \{\
  "page_index": 12,\
  "ocr_engine": "tesseract",\
  "ocr_language": ["eng", "por"],\
  "dpi": 300,\
  "deskewed": true,\
  "mean_word_confidence": 86.4,\
  "low_confidence_word_count": 38,\
  "hocr_path": "ocr/page_0012.hocr",\
  "tsv_path": "ocr/page_0012.tsv",\
  "failure_flags": ["marginalia", "faint_text", "two_column_layout"]\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Never let OCR text silently become \'93the document.\'94 It is a derived artifact.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Tables
\f1\b0 \
Tables need their own evidence lane.\
Camelot and Tabula are good when the PDF is text-based. Camelot explicitly does not work with image-based/scanned PDFs ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://camelot-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/faq.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Camelot FAQ}}); Tabula is likewise a table extractor for PDFs and supports page/area-based extraction via CLI ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/tabulapdf/tabula-java"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 tabula-java}}). Docling can detect tables and export structured document representations, including JSON/Markdown, and is built around layout/table recognition models ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.docling.ai/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Docling}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docling-project.github.io/docling/reference/docling_document/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Docling document reference}}). Marker/Surya can be better for difficult layouts and scanned material, but treat results as proposals, especially for multi-page tables and merged headers ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://builders.mozilla.org/project/marker/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Marker overview}}).\
Your table sidecar should preserve cells, not only CSV:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \{\
  "table_id": "tbl_p045_01",\
  "page_start": 45,\
  "page_end": 46,\
  "caption": "Reported outcomes by year",\
  "extractor": "camelot_lattice",\
  "review_state": "needs_review",\
  "cells": [\
    \{\
      "row": 0,\
      "col": 0,\
      "text": "Year",\
      "bbox": [72, 120, 130, 140],\
      "is_header": true,\
      "confidence": 0.94\
    \}\
  ],\
  "source_crop": "crops/tbl_p045_01.png"\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 For public-facing evidence, table values should be clickable back to the page crop.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Citations, Bibliography, Footnotes
\f1\b0 \
Use 
\f0\b GROBID
\f1\b0  wherever the document is scholarly or citation-heavy. It can process a full PDF into TEI containing header, body, and bibliographical section, and it supports coordinates for structures such as references, bibliographic entries, section heads, paragraphs, notes, figures, and tables ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://grobid.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Grobid-service/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 GROBID service docs}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://grobid.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Coordinates-in-PDF/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 GROBID coordinates}}).\
For books, NGO reports, manuals, and religious/theological material, GROBID will not be enough. Add custom extractors for:\

